<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Rabih az-Zubayr's state</h1><p class="st-meta">1879 CE – 1900 CE · capital Dikwa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The conquest state of the Sudanese warlord Rabih, which overran Bornu before French forces destroyed it at Kousseri in 1900.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih az-Zubayr</a> had served the slave-trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-zubayr-rahma-mansur">Zubayr Rahma Mansur</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahr-al-ghazal">Bahr al-Ghazal</a>. When Egyptian forces crushed al-Zubayr's son Sulayman in 1879, Rabih led an army of several hundred riflemen west into the borderlands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-dar-al-kuti">Dar al Kuti</a> and the Chari basin, where he built a new power.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih</a> ruled as a military chief, with a disciplined army of slave soldiers armed with rifles, raiding widely for captives. He made Muhammad al-Sanusi of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-dar-al-kuti">Dar al Kuti</a> his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, attacked the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-baguirmi">Kingdom of Baguirmi</a> and in 1893 conquered the ancient <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire</a>, ruling from a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dikwa">Dikwa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French columns converged on the Chari, and on 22 April 1900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih</a> was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kousseri">Kousseri</a>, where the French commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amedee-francois-lamy">Lamy</a> also died. His son Fadlallah was killed in 1901, and the lands were divided among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rabih-az-zubayrs-state.jpg" alt="Rabih az-Zubayr's state" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: Arrantzaleak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rabih_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1879 CE – 1900 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dikwa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rabih az-Zubayr, Fadlallah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/chad">Chad</a>, <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a href="/countries/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rabih's empire, Rabih az-Zubayr, State of Rabih, Rabih az-Zubayr's empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=rabih-az-zubayrs-state" aria-label="Rabih az-Zubayr's state on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5062 1729 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rabih az-Zubayr's state lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5382 1985L5399 1946M5382 1985L5401 2038M5382 1985L5054 728M5382 1985L5445 2008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5399 1946h0M5401 2038h0M5054 728h0M5445 2008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5382 1985h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5382 1985h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dikwa. <a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=rabih-az-zubayrs-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
